# Verity — environment template. Copy to .env and fill in.
# .env is gitignored; never commit real values.
#
# Only variables the platform currently reads are listed. Legacy `S3_*` names
# are intentionally unsupported; the portable storage driver reads
# `VERITY_S3_*`. `OPENAI_*` is now an optional OpenAI-compatible agent binding.
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY was on that removed list and is BACK, under exactly
# that rule: the plywood client needs files to persist, so a storage driver was
# bound on 2026-08-28 and it needs the key. That is what "genuinely needs it"
# was written to describe.

# Runtime connection. Port 6543 is the pooler in TRANSACTION mode.
#
# This was 5432 (SESSION mode) on the stated grounds that "withTenant opens an
# interactive transaction to hold the tenant GUC, which needs a session-pinned
# connection; transaction-mode pooling cannot start one." That is wrong, and it
# took production down with:
#
#   FATAL: (EMAXCONNSESSION) max clients reached in session mode
#   - max clients are limited to pool_size: 15
#
# Session mode pins one server connection per *client* for the client's whole
# life, and the pool is 15 for the entire project. Every serverless instance
# held up to `connection_limit` of them until the pool was gone.
#
# Transaction mode pins a connection for the duration of a transaction and
# releases it at COMMIT — which is precisely what an interactive transaction
# needs, and why BEGIN/COMMIT is supported there. What transaction mode cannot
# carry is session state held *between* transactions. Nothing here does: the
# tenant GUC is set with `set_config(..., true)`, transaction-local by
# construction, and the only lock taken is pg_advisory_xact_lock, likewise
# transaction-scoped. Verified against this database before the switch — the GUC
# round-trips inside withTenant and RLS filters correctly under concurrency.
#
# `pgbouncer=true` is required: it stops Prisma using named prepared statements,
# which do not survive a pooler handing the connection to another transaction.
#
# `connection_limit` is per process, not per project. Keep it small — a
# serverless deployment runs many processes, and each one holds its own pool.
#
# MUST be a role created NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS:
#   CREATE ROLE verity_app LOGIN NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS;
# PostgreSQL does not enforce row-level security for a SUPERUSER or BYPASSRLS
# role, and Supabase's default `postgres` role has rolbypassrls = true, so using
# it here would disable tenant isolation while every test still passed.
# assertRlsEnforceable() refuses to start on such a connection.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://verity_app.<project-ref>:<app-password>@aws-1-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?connection_limit=5&pgbouncer=true&pool_timeout=20"

# Migrations only. Privileged; bypasses RLS by design. Never used at runtime.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:<db-password>@db.<project-ref>.supabase.co:5432/postgres"

# Interactive-transaction budget for withTenant (tenancy.ts) — the single
# chokepoint every tenant-scoped read/write passes through. Optional; a
# deployment on a slower or more distant database may need to raise these.
# VERITY_TX_TIMEOUT_MS="15000"
# VERITY_TX_MAX_WAIT_MS="5000"

# Public client configuration.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Private signing material for membership and browser-session cookies. The
# public anon key is never a signing-secret fallback.
VERITY_SESSION_SECRET=""
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET remains a supported Supabase-specific alias.
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=""
# How a Supabase session is confirmed on each page. "server" (default) asks the auth server every time, so a revoked session ends at
# once; "local" verifies the token here (no round trip) and a revoked session lives until its access token expires (about an hour).
# VERITY_SUPABASE_SESSION_CHECK="server"

# Alternative enterprise identity profile (no Supabase auth required).
# VERITY_AUTH_PROVIDER="oidc"
# VERITY_OIDC_ISSUER="https://idp.example.com/realms/acme"
# VERITY_OIDC_CLIENT_ID="verity-web"
# VERITY_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI="https://verity.example.com/api/auth/oidc/callback"
# VERITY_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduled work (ADR-015, ADR-016)
#
# The deployment host calls POST/GET /api/scheduled on a schedule and presents
# this as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>`. Vercel Cron sets that header itself
# once CRON_SECRET is an environment variable on the project.
#
# WITHOUT IT NOTHING RUNS, deliberately. The route returns 503 rather than
# running unauthenticated: a scheduler endpoint with no secret is a way to make
# the platform do work on demand from outside, and "the variable was missing" is
# not a reason to become that.
#
# Generate a fresh one per environment; do not reuse across projects:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
CRON_SECRET=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# File storage (bound 2026-08-28)
#
# The platform owns the RECORD of a file and delegates the bytes to a driver.
# Supabase Storage is bound because Supabase already holds authentication and
# the database — same project, same region, no new vendor. The plywood client is
# the requirement that made binding one legitimate: an LR scan or a signed
# delivery receipt is Evidence, and Evidence with no file is a row claiming a
# photograph exists.
#
# Leave these blank and the platform still runs. `files.ts` refuses at the point
# of use with E_STORAGE_UNAVAILABLE rather than failing at boot, so a deployment
# that never touches a file is a valid deployment.
#
# The service-role key bypasses RLS and never leaves the server. It is used only
# to mint short-lived signed URLs for one object at a time.
SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""
SUPABASE_MEDIA_BUCKET="verity-media"

# Alternative S3-compatible storage profile. Use these exact names.
# VERITY_STORAGE_DRIVER="s3"
# VERITY_S3_BUCKET="verity-media"
# VERITY_S3_REGION="us-east-1"
# VERITY_S3_ENDPOINT="http://localhost:9000"
# VERITY_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
# VERITY_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
# VERITY_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE="true"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional OpenAI-compatible agent provider (OpenAI, Groq, or compatible API).
OPENAI_API_KEY=""
OPENAI_BASE_URL=""
OPENAI_MODEL=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telemetry (optional). Sentry initialises only when all three are set.
#
# Audit finding F-05: the organisation and project were hard-coded to a legacy
# VEDA project, so Verity's errors reported into a different product. Which
# organisation a deployment reports into is a deployment fact, not a source
# fact, so it lives here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=

# Fraction of transactions traced. Defaults to 0.1; was effectively 1.0.
SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=

# Audit finding F-06. Setting this creates an UNAUTHENTICATED endpoint at the
# given path that forwards request bodies to Sentry's ingest, to evade ad
# blockers. Leave unset unless a deployment has accepted that trade.
SENTRY_TUNNEL_ROUTE=
